─── 2. Stipple-pattern intermediate colors ───────────────────────────────

  Pure A               [48;2;255;140;50m            [0m
  ▓  (≈75% A, 25% B)   [48;2;40;80;200m[38;2;255;140;50m▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓▓[0m
  ▒  (≈50% A, 50% B)   [48;2;40;80;200m[38;2;255;140;50m▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒▒[0m
  ░  (≈25% A, 75% B)   [48;2;40;80;200m[38;2;255;140;50m░░░░░░░░░░░░[0m
  Pure B               [48;2;40;80;200m            [0m

  Truecolor midpoint reference (computed (A+B)/2):
  Midpoint             [48;2;147;110;125m            [0m

  Compare the ▒ row above to the midpoint row — perceptually similar,
  but the ▒ version uses ONLY the two source colors A and B. 16-color
  terminals faked colors they didn't have this way; with truecolor, the
  same trick adds 1–2 effective bits of perceived range by spatially
  dithering between exactly-displayable colors.

─── Bonus: stipple gradient vs. truecolor gradient ───────────────────────

  5-step gradient using only A, B, and density (no color interpolation):
  [48;2;255;140;50m    [0m[48;2;40;80;200m[38;2;255;140;50m▓▓▓▓[0m[48;2;40;80;200m[38;2;255;140;50m▒▒▒▒[0m[48;2;40;80;200m[38;2;255;140;50m░░░░[0m[48;2;40;80;200m    [0m

  5-step gradient using truecolor RGB interpolation:
  [48;2;255;140;50m    [0m[48;2;201;125;87m    [0m[48;2;147;110;125m    [0m[48;2;93;95;162m    [0m[48;2;40;80;200m    [0m

  At normal viewing distance the two rows should look very close.
  Up close, the stipple version reveals its character-density pattern;
  from a foot or two away, the eye averages each cell into an apparent
  intermediate color for free.

